This specification applies to:

- Classification Architecture;
- Classification Concepts;
- Classification Model;
- Classification Elements;
- Classification Schema;
- Classification Lifecycle;
- Classification Validation;
- Classification Governance;
- Classification Relationships;
- Classification Constraints;
- Classification Extensibility;
- Classification Dictionary;
- Classification assignments.

It also applies to the classification of documents using the canonical Classification model.

It does not specify:

- implementation technologies;
- programming languages;
- storage mechanisms;
- repository structures;
- serialization formats;
- user-interface technologies;
- software architectures.

---

## 5.3. Technology Independence

Classification shall remain independent of implementation technologies.

The meaning of a Classification Element shall not depend on:

- programming language;
- storage technology;
- repository;
- serialization format;
- documentation tool;
- rendering engine.

The architectural concepts of the Framework are explicitly required to remain independent from implementation technologies.

---

## 5.5. Explicit Architecture

Classification semantics shall be explicitly defined.

Critical semantics shall not depend exclusively on:

- directory structure;
- filename conventions;
- implementation behavior;
- undocumented assumptions.

The Framework favors explicit architectural relationships over implicit conventions.

---

## 6.18. R-018 — Responsibility Separation

Classification shall not assume responsibilities belonging to other Documentation Framework components.

In particular, Classification shall not use its hierarchy as a substitute for:

- Document Identity;
- Metadata;
- Lifecycle;
- Governance;
- Registry;
- Relationships;
- Knowledge Graph.

---

## 6.19. R-019 — Lifecycle Compliance

Classification Elements shall evolve according to the Classification Lifecycle defined by DF-CLS-006.

Lifecycle transitions shall be:

- explicit;
- valid;
- governed;
- traceable.

---

## 6.23. R-023 — Validation

Classification shall support architectural validation.

Validation shall determine whether Classification conforms to:

- the Classification Schema;
- Classification Constraints;
- applicable relationships;
- lifecycle requirements;
- semantic requirements.

---

## 6.28. R-028 — Controlled Extension

Classification extensions shall conform to DF-CLS-011.

Extensions shall:

- preserve semantic compatibility;
- preserve architectural consistency;
- respect existing constraints;
- preserve existing responsibilities;
- be subject to applicable governance.

---

## 6.31. R-031 — Dictionary Compliance

Every canonical Classification Element shall possess an authoritative Dictionary definition in DF-CLS-013.

Dictionary definitions shall include sufficient information to establish:

- canonical terminology;
- semantic meaning;
- lifecycle status;
- relevant relationships;
- applicable architectural context.

---

# 10. Validation Requirements

Classification Validation shall evaluate architectural compliance.

Validation shall evaluate, as applicable:

- structural validity;
- semantic validity;
- relationship validity;
- cardinality;
- constraints;
- lifecycle state;
- Dictionary consistency.

Validation shall not:

- redefine Classification;
- authorize changes;
- replace governance;
- modify the Classification model.

---

# 11. Governance Requirements

Classification Governance shall:

- authorize significant Classification evolution;
- approve new canonical elements;
- approve structural changes;
- approve relationship changes;
- control deprecation and retirement;
- preserve architectural integrity;
- maintain traceability.

Governance decisions shall be documented.

---

# 12. Relationship Requirements

Classification Relationships shall be explicit.

A relationship shall define:

- relationship type;
- source;
- target;
- semantic meaning;
- applicable cardinality;
- applicable constraints.

Hierarchical relationships shall be distinguished from non-hierarchical relationships.

---

# 13. Constraint Requirements

All Classification Constraints defined by DF-CLS-010 shall be satisfied.

Constraints may govern:

- structural organization;
- hierarchy;
- relationships;
- cardinality;
- lifecycle;
- semantic consistency;
- assignments.

Constraint violations shall be detectable through Classification Validation.

---

# 14. Extensibility Requirements

Classification extensions shall:

- preserve existing Classification semantics;
- preserve existing architectural boundaries;
- avoid unnecessary duplication;
- respect Classification Constraints;
- preserve compatibility where possible;
- undergo applicable governance.

An extension shall not redefine existing canonical Classification concepts without an explicit architectural decision.

---

# 15. Dictionary Requirements

Every canonical Classification Element shall possess one authoritative Dictionary Entry.

Dictionary Entries shall provide, at minimum:

- identifier;
- canonical name;
- definition;
- semantic meaning;
- classification context;
- lifecycle status;
- version;
- revision;
- related elements where applicable;
- applicable constraints where applicable.

The Dictionary defines terminology and authoritative meanings; it does not replace the Classification Schema or Validation mechanisms.

This follows the established Dictionary pattern in the Documentation Framework, where each canonical element has one authoritative entry and terminology remains technology independent and traceable.

---

# 16. Conformance Levels

This specification defines two levels of conformance.

## 16.1. Core Conformance

A Classification implementation conforms at the Core level when it satisfies every mandatory requirement defined by this specification.

Core Conformance includes:

- Classification Architecture compliance;
- Classification Model compliance;
- Element compliance;
- Schema compliance;
- semantic consistency;
- relationship compliance;
- constraint compliance;
- validation support;
- lifecycle compliance;
- Dictionary consistency.

---

## 16.2. Extended Conformance

An implementation conforms at the Extended level when it:

- satisfies Core Conformance;
- supports Classification extensibility;
- supports Classification governance;
- supports Classification lifecycle management;
- supports Classification evolution traceability.

---

## 16.1. Core Conformance

A Classification implementation conforms at the Core level when it satisfies every mandatory requirement defined by this specification.

Core Conformance includes:

- Classification Architecture compliance;
- Classification Model compliance;
- Element compliance;
- Schema compliance;
- semantic consistency;
- relationship compliance;
- constraint compliance;
- validation support;
- lifecycle compliance;
- Dictionary consistency.

---

## 16.2. Extended Conformance

An implementation conforms at the Extended level when it:

- satisfies Core Conformance;
- supports Classification extensibility;
- supports Classification governance;
- supports Classification lifecycle management;
- supports Classification evolution traceability.

---

# 17. Compliance Assessment

Compliance shall evaluate:

- architectural conformance;
- conceptual conformance;
- semantic conformance;
- structural conformance;
- relationship conformance;
- constraint conformance;
- lifecycle conformance;
- validation conformance;
- governance conformance;
- Dictionary conformance;
- extensibility conformance where applicable.

Compliance assessment shall remain independent of implementation technologies.

---

# 20. Conformance

A Classification implementation conforms to this specification when:

- all mandatory requirements are satisfied;
- Classification conforms to the Classification Architecture;
- Classification uses valid concepts and elements;
- Classification conforms to the Classification Schema;
- Classification semantics are preserved;
- Classification relationships are valid;
- Classification Constraints are satisfied;
- Classification Validation is supported;
- Classification Lifecycle requirements are respected;
- Classification Governance requirements are respected;
- canonical Classification terminology is maintained;
- Classification evolution remains traceable.

---

# 25. Architectural Dependencies

The Classification component is defined within the architectural context established by the Documentation Framework.

Its conceptual foundation depends upon the Framework Architecture documents, including:

- DF-ARC-001 — Architecture Overview;
- DF-ARC-002 — Architectural Principles;
- DF-ARC-003 — Architectural Concepts;
- DF-ARC-004 — Architectural Layers;
- DF-ARC-005 — Architectural Components;
- DF-ARC-006 — Terminology;
- DF-ARC-007 — Component Documentation Structure.

The Framework establishes Classification as an architectural concept alongside Metadata, Document Identity and other components.

---

# 31. Implementation Independence

This specification does not prescribe how Classification is implemented.

A conforming implementation may use:

- files;
- databases;
- APIs;
- configuration systems;
- in-memory structures;
- other mechanisms.

Such implementation choices shall not alter the canonical Classification semantics.

---

# 32. Evolution of the Specification

The Classification Specification shall evolve through controlled architectural governance.

A modification to this Specification shall consider its impact on:

- Classification Architecture;
- Classification Concepts;
- Classification Model;
- Classification Elements;
- Classification Schema;
- Classification Lifecycle;
- Classification Validation;
- Classification Governance;
- Classification Relationships;
- Classification Constraints;
- Classification Extensibility;
- Classification Dictionary;
- Classification Examples.

Existing requirements shall not be silently redefined.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this document is to provide concrete examples of Classification usage.

The examples support:

- understanding the Classification model;
- understanding Classification terminology;
- illustrating Classification structures;
- demonstrating Classification assignments;
- demonstrating relationships;
- demonstrating validation;
- demonstrating lifecycle transitions;
- demonstrating governance;
- demonstrating extensibility;
- illustrating correct and incorrect Classification practices.

---

# 5. Architectural Purpose

The Metadata component provides standardized descriptive information that characterizes documents independently of their content.

Metadata enables every document to become:

- self-describing;
- uniquely identifiable;
- semantically classifiable;
- governable;
- discoverable;
- traceable;
- automatable.

Metadata does not replace document content.

Instead, it complements document content by providing architectural information about the document itself.

---

# 6. Responsibilities

The Metadata component is responsible for:

- describing documents through standardized Metadata;
- providing descriptive information for architectural components;
- supporting document identification;
- supporting document classification;
- supporting governance processes;
- supporting discovery mechanisms;
- supporting architectural traceability;
- supporting automation activities.

Metadata shall not perform responsibilities assigned to other architectural components.

---

# 8. Architectural Dependencies

## 8.1. Depends On

The Metadata component depends only upon the Foundation and Architecture layers.

It shall not depend upon Governance, Discovery, Automation or Integration.

---

## 8.2. Required By

Metadata provides architectural support for:

- Document Identity;
- Classification;
- Workflow;
- Policies;
- Baselines;
- Registry;
- Relationships;
- Knowledge Graph;
- Validation;
- Automation;
- AI Integration.

These components consume Metadata but do not own it.

---

# 14. Conformance

An implementation conforms to the Metadata Architecture when:

- Metadata is maintained independently of document content;
- Metadata responsibilities remain isolated from other components;
- Metadata follows the Documentation Framework terminology;
- Metadata respects the Documentation Layer boundaries;
- Metadata remains technology independent;
- Metadata is extensible without architectural disruption.

---

# 5. Architectural Purpose

The Templates component provides reusable structural definitions for documentation.

A template establishes a standardized organization that may define, where applicable:

* document sections;
* structural elements;
* metadata locations;
* classification locations;
* references;
* content placeholders;
* instructions;
* structural rules;
* structural constraints.

Templates allow recurring documentation structures to be defined once and reused consistently.

The component therefore contributes to:

* structural consistency;
* documentation reuse;
* predictable organization;
* maintainability;
* validation;
* documentation generation;
* controlled structural evolution.

---

# 6. Responsibilities

The Templates component is responsible for:

* defining template architecture;
* defining template concepts;
* defining the template model;
* defining template elements;
* defining template structures;
* defining template schemas;
* defining template lifecycle;
* defining template validation;
* defining template governance;
* defining template relationships;
* defining template constraints;
* defining template extensibility;
* defining reusable documentation structures;
* defining authoritative template terminology;
* supporting standardized documentation creation;
* supporting structural conformance.

Templates shall provide structural definitions without redefining concepts owned by other architectural components.

---

# 7. Non-Responsibilities

The Templates component shall not:

* assign permanent document identities;
* define document identity semantics;
* define Metadata semantics;
* define Metadata ownership;
* classify documents;
* define classification semantics;
* manage document repositories;
* manage document storage;
* manage document permissions;
* manage document workflow;
* govern organizational policies;
* maintain document registries;
* define knowledge graph semantics;
* implement document generation;
* prescribe implementation technologies.

Templates may provide structures for representing information owned by other components, but ownership of that information remains with the corresponding component.

---

# 8. Architectural Boundaries

The Templates component is bounded by the distinction between **document structure** and the semantic or operational responsibilities of other components.

Templates define how documentation may be structurally organized.

They do not determine:

* what permanently identifies a document;
* what metadata means;
* how documents are classified;
* where document instances are stored;
* how documents progress through workflows;
* how documents are governed operationally;
* how documents are discovered or indexed.

This separation preserves component independence and prevents structural definitions from becoming substitutes for other architectural responsibilities.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this roadmap is to define:

- the implementation sequence of the Classification component;
- the progressive introduction of classification capabilities;
- the evolution of classification structures;
- dependencies and integration with other architectural components;
- long-term classification governance.

---

# 3. Scope

This roadmap covers:

- classification architecture;
- classification concepts;
- classification models;
- classification schemes;
- classification rules;
- classification validation;
- classification governance;
- classification extensibility.

It does not define:

- Document Identity;
- Metadata;
- Templates;
- document content;
- document storage;
- document workflows.

These responsibilities belong to other Documentation Framework components.

---

# 6. Design Objectives

The Metadata Schema has the following objectives.

- provide a canonical Metadata structure;
- support structural consistency;
- enable Metadata reuse;
- facilitate extensibility;
- preserve compatibility;
- support specialized Metadata Profiles.

---

# 3. Scope

This document specifies:

- the purpose of the Metadata Dictionary;
- the organization of dictionary entries;
- Metadata Element definitions;
- Metadata Element attributes;
- dictionary maintenance principles.

It does not define:

- Metadata architecture;
- Metadata schemas;
- Metadata profiles;
- validation rules;
- implementation formats.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this roadmap is to define.

- the implementation sequence of the Metadata component;
- the progressive introduction of Metadata capabilities;
- the dependencies with other architectural components;
- the expected deliverables for each implementation stage.

---

# 3. Scope

This roadmap covers every aspect related to Metadata.

Including.

- Metadata model;
- Metadata schema;
- Metadata validation;
- Metadata evolution;
- Metadata governance;
- Metadata automation.

It does not cover Registry, Workflow, Baselines or Knowledge Graph except where Metadata acts as a dependency.

---

# 3. Scope

This document defines Classification conceptual meaning and its current working source/owner boundaries. The ten received detailed exclusions remain:

- the formal Classification Model;
- the structure of Classification Elements;
- the Classification Schema;
- lifecycle states;
- validation rules;
- governance rules;
- relationship types;
- constraints;
- extensibility mechanisms;
- implementation details.

Architectural/conceptual requirements may refer to those subjects without completing their detailed owner contracts. No physical storage fields, serialization, universal cardinality, complete Class vocabulary, Metadata Element/Profile registration or installed executor is created. The intended Model is still unresolved; source references and selected views cannot substitute for it. Missing or conflicting authority is recorded rather than converted into unconditional success or an observed violation.

---

# 8. Concept Boundaries

The Metadata component defines descriptive concepts only.

It does not define:

- document identity;
- document classification;
- workflow states;
- governance rules;
- relationships between documents;
- validation procedures.

These concepts belong to their respective architectural components.

---

# 15. Metadata Element Identification

Every Metadata Element shall possess a permanent architectural identity.

This identity shall remain stable regardless of:

- Metadata Schemas;
- Metadata Profiles;
- implementation technologies;
- serialization formats.

The identification mechanism itself is defined separately from the Metadata Element architecture.

---

# 9. Lifecycle Events

Typical lifecycle events include:

- Metadata creation;
- Metadata refinement;
- Metadata approval;
- Metadata publication;
- Metadata extension;
- Metadata deprecation;
- Metadata retirement.

Each event shall be traceable.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of Metadata Validation is to determine whether Metadata conforms to the architectural specifications of the Documentation Framework.

Metadata Validation ensures that Metadata is:

- structurally correct;
- semantically consistent;
- architecturally compliant;
- internally coherent.

Validation protects the integrity of Metadata without altering its meaning.

---

# 3. Scope

This document specifies:

- Metadata validation principles;
- validation categories;
- validation responsibilities;
- validation outcomes;
- architectural constraints.

It does not define:

- implementation algorithms;
- validation technologies;
- validation tools;
- serialization validation;
- application-specific business rules.

---

# 5. Validation Objectives

Metadata Validation has the following objectives.

- verify Metadata completeness;
- verify structural correctness;
- verify semantic consistency;
- verify profile compliance;
- verify architectural conformance.

---

# 7. Validation Categories

Metadata Validation is organized into complementary categories.

---

## 7.1. Structural Validation

Verifies whether Metadata conforms to the applicable Metadata Schema.

Examples include:

- required Metadata Elements;
- structural organization;
- cardinality;
- Metadata grouping.

---

## 7.2. Profile Validation

Verifies compliance with the applicable Metadata Profile.

Examples include:

- mandatory Metadata;
- optional Metadata;
- conditional Metadata;
- profile-specific constraints.

---

## 7.3. Semantic Validation

Verifies that Metadata preserves architectural meaning.

Semantic Validation confirms that:

- Metadata Elements are correctly used;
- Metadata Values correspond to their Metadata Elements;
- semantic definitions are preserved.

---

## 7.4. Consistency Validation

Verifies internal consistency among Metadata Elements.

Examples include:

- contradictory Metadata;
- incompatible Metadata combinations;
- duplicated Metadata.

---

## 7.5. Referential Validation

Verifies references established by Metadata.

Examples include:

- referenced documents;
- referenced baselines;
- referenced versions;
- referenced identifiers.

The existence of referenced resources may be validated by other architectural components.

---

# 8. Validation Rules

Validation is performed through Validation Rules.

Each Validation Rule shall possess:

- unique identity;
- purpose;
- scope;
- validation criterion;
- expected outcome.

Validation Rules are defined independently from implementation.

---

# 13. Validation Responsibilities

Metadata Validation is responsible for:

- evaluating Metadata;
- reporting conformance;
- identifying inconsistencies;
- supporting Metadata quality.

Metadata Validation is not responsible for:

- correcting Metadata;
- generating Metadata;
- interpreting document content;
- approving Metadata.

---

# 15. Validation Lifecycle

Validation is performed whenever Metadata is evaluated.

Typical validation events include:

- Metadata creation;
- Metadata modification;
- Metadata publication;
- Metadata migration;
- Metadata import;
- Metadata synchronization.

The Metadata component does not prescribe when validation shall occur.

That decision belongs to consuming architectural components.

---

# 17. Conformance

A Metadata Validation implementation conforms to this specification when:

- validation is performed against the canonical Metadata architecture;
- Metadata remains unchanged by validation;
- Validation Rules are explicit;
- Validation Results are reproducible;
- validation preserves Metadata semantics.

---

# 7. Governance Responsibilities

Metadata Governance is responsible for:

- evaluating Metadata changes;
- approving Metadata evolution;
- preserving architectural integrity;
- managing Metadata deprecation;
- authorizing Metadata publication;
- maintaining Metadata consistency.

Metadata Governance is not responsible for:

- validating Metadata;
- generating Metadata;
- implementing Metadata;
- storing Metadata;
- interpreting document content.

---

# 8. Governance Decisions

Governance decisions include:

- introduction of new Metadata Elements;
- modification of Metadata Schemas;
- approval of Metadata Profiles;
- Metadata deprecation;
- Metadata retirement;
- Metadata publication.

Every governance decision shall be documented.

---

# 9. Governance Criteria

Governance decisions shall evaluate whether a proposed change:

- conforms to the Documentation Framework architecture;
- preserves semantic consistency;
- maintains backward compatibility whenever possible;
- avoids duplication;
- respects component responsibilities;
- supports long-term architectural stability.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of Metadata Governance is to ensure that Metadata evolves in a controlled, consistent and traceable manner.

Metadata Governance establishes:

- governance principles;
- governance responsibilities;
- governance processes;
- governance decisions;
- governance constraints.

---

# 3. Scope

This document specifies the governance of:

- Metadata Elements;
- Metadata Schemas;
- Metadata Profiles;
- Metadata Definitions;
- Metadata evolution.

It does not specify:

- document governance;
- workflow governance;
- repository governance;
- organizational governance;
- implementation governance.

---

# 5. Governance Objectives

Metadata Governance has the following objectives.

- preserve Metadata integrity;
- preserve semantic consistency;
- manage Metadata evolution;
- control Metadata changes;
- ensure architectural compliance;
- support long-term interoperability.

---

# 13. Governance Policies

Metadata Governance applies policies concerning:

- Metadata introduction;
- Metadata modification;
- Metadata extension;
- Metadata versioning;
- Metadata deprecation;
- Metadata retirement.

Policy definitions are outside the scope of this document.

This document defines the architectural framework in which those policies operate.

---

# 17. Governance Artifacts

Metadata Governance may produce governance artifacts including:

- architectural decisions;
- approval records;
- deprecation notices;
- retirement notices;
- impact assessments;
- governance reports.

The representation of these artifacts is implementation independent.

---

# 19. Conformance

A Metadata Governance implementation conforms to this specification when:

- governance decisions are explicit;
- Metadata evolution follows controlled governance;
- lifecycle transitions are authorized;
- governance preserves Metadata integrity;
- governance maintains architectural consistency.

---

# 13. Constraint Violations

A Metadata artifact violates this specification whenever one or more constraints are not satisfied.

Typical violations include:

- duplicated Metadata Elements;
- conflicting Metadata definitions;
- semantic inconsistencies;
- invalid structural organization;
- circular dependencies;
- unauthorized Metadata evolution.

The identification of violations belongs to Metadata Validation.

The resolution of violations belongs to Metadata Governance.

---

# 16. Conformance

A Metadata implementation conforms to this specification when:

- all applicable Metadata Constraints are respected;
- Metadata concepts preserve their canonical meaning;
- structural organization conforms to the Metadata Schema;
- Metadata evolution follows governance rules;
- architectural dependencies remain valid;
- constraint violations are detectable through Metadata Validation.

---

# 5. Extensibility Objectives

Metadata Extensibility has the following objectives.

- accommodate future architectural requirements;
- support new document categories;
- enable Metadata specialization;
- preserve interoperability;
- avoid architectural fragmentation.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this document is to provide architectural guidance for the effective use of the Metadata component.

It promotes:

- architectural consistency;
- semantic clarity;
- maintainability;
- interoperability;
- long-term stability.

---

# 3. Scope

This document provides recommendations for:

- Metadata design;
- Metadata evolution;
- Metadata organization;
- Metadata governance;
- Metadata maintenance.

It does not define mandatory architectural requirements.

Normative requirements are defined by the Metadata Specification and related architectural documents.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this document is to:

- illustrate Metadata concepts;
- demonstrate the interaction between Metadata artifacts;
- provide architectural examples;
- clarify the intended use of Metadata specifications.

---

# 3. Scope

This document contains examples of:

- Metadata Elements;
- Metadata Schemas;
- Metadata Profiles;
- Metadata Instances;
- Metadata Relationships;
- Metadata Validation;
- Metadata Governance.

The examples are implementation independent.

---

